1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,338
ELCODE ProcureKit is a multi-tenant procure-to-pay platform.

2
00:00:04,338 --> 00:00:12,720
This is the full slice: a requisition, its approval, a purchase order, a goods receipt, and a three-way invoice match.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:21,170
Everything runs on a local Docker Compose stack: five Go services, Postgres, Mongo, Redis, RabbitMQ, MinIO, and OpenTelemetry.

4
00:00:21,170 --> 00:00:22,822
The web app talks only to the gateway.

5
00:00:22,822 --> 00:00:25,188
We start as the admin of Acme.

6
00:00:25,188 --> 00:00:34,398
The tenant switcher proves isolation: switching to Glow changes the vendor list, because every table is protected by row-level security.

7
00:00:34,398 --> 00:00:36,035
Back in Acme, we raise a requisition.

8
00:00:36,035 --> 00:00:43,149
The wizard takes four steps: department and purpose, vendor, line items with quantity and price, then a review.

9
00:00:43,149 --> 00:00:49,834
The seeded vendor is E2E Acme Supplies, one line item, and the totals are derived, not typed.

10
00:00:49,834 --> 00:00:53,391
The requisition is submitted and enters the approval chain.

11
00:00:53,391 --> 00:00:58,455
Now the rule that matters: self-approval is forbidden, even for admins.

12
00:00:58,455 --> 00:01:01,805
So we sign out and sign back in as the approver in the same tenant.

13
00:01:01,805 --> 00:01:07,479
The requester's view shows no action buttons; the approver can act.

14
00:01:07,479 --> 00:01:08,379
Approved.

15
00:01:07,716 --> 00:01:16,766
Next, the requisition is converted to a purchase order through the gateway API, because the convert screen does not exist yet.

16
00:01:16,766 --> 00:01:18,668
That gap is tracked as an issue.

17
00:01:18,668 --> 00:01:23,478
The PO moves from draft to issued, then to acknowledged by the vendor.

18
00:01:23,478 --> 00:01:28,815
Only then can we record a goods receipt, and the receipt claims exactly the ordered quantity.

19
00:01:28,815 --> 00:01:36,525
Finally the invoice, created against the PO, with a PNG attachment uploaded straight to MinIO through a presigned URL.

20
00:01:36,525 --> 00:01:40,723
The attachment has to reach ready before the match will run.

21
00:01:40,723 --> 00:01:44,062
The three-way match compares requisition, receipt, and invoice.

22
00:01:44,062 --> 00:01:48,683
Quantities and prices line up, and the verdict is matched.

23
00:01:48,683 --> 00:01:54,480
Every step writes an audit event and publishes to an outbox; the worker consumes it into read models.

24
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:59,693
Prometheus scrapes the services' internal metrics.

25
00:01:59,693 --> 00:02:05,695
In Grafana, the eProcurement overview shows outbox lag and dead-letter depth.

26
00:02:05,695 --> 00:02:09,395
Lag returning to zero means the worker has drained the queue.

27
00:02:09,395 --> 00:02:12,420
A rising dead-letter count is what the alerts watch.

28
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:17,767
Jaeger shows the same request as one trace.

29
00:02:17,767 --> 00:02:26,746
The gateway span parents the gRPC call into procurement, the database query, the outbox publish, and the consumer on the worker.

30
00:02:26,746 --> 00:02:30,320
One purchase order, one trace, end to end.

31
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:33,330
ProcureKit is built by ELCODE.

32
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:37,440
The code, the runbook, and the demo script are in the repository.
